Chapter: Phase Allocator

Section: Primitives

  pitch : Integer -> Integer between 0 and 4294967295
  pitch (size) =
   let p = __heap-save
   in let advanced = __heap-advance size
   in p

  strike : Integer -> Nothing
  strike (start) =
   __heap-restore start

  build : Integer -> Integer
  build (size) =
   let p = __heap-save
   in let deck-init = __deck-set p
   in let advanced = __heap-advance size
   in p

  seal : Integer -> Nothing
  seal (start) =
   __heap-advance 0

  deck-record : a -> a
  deck-record (x) =
   x

Section: Mountain Base

  init-phase-allocator : Integer
  init-phase-allocator =
   let base = __heap-save
   in let deck-init = __deck-set base
   in base

Section: Phase Measurement

  PhaseStart = record {
   bivy-origin : Integer between 0 and 4294967295,
   deck-origin : Integer between 0 and 4294967295
  }

 A phase carries the name it is known by. Without it the metrics list is
 positional, and a reader has to map DECK-4 onto a phase by counting
 `phase-start` calls in `opening.codex` -- which is how one entry came
 to hold two ratios that disagreed by a factor of four with nobody able to
 settle which phase either described. A measurement that needs a decoder
 ring is a measurement that will be misquoted.

  PhaseMetrics = record {
   label : Text,
   bivy-origin : Integer between 0 and 4294967295,
   bivy-hwm : Integer between 0 and 4294967295,
   deck-origin : Integer between 0 and 4294967295,
   deck-end : Integer between 0 and 4294967295
  }

  phase-start : PhaseStart
  phase-start =
   let bivy = pitch 0
   in PhaseStart { bivy-origin = bivy, deck-origin = __deck-pos }

  phase-measure : PhaseStart, Text -> PhaseMetrics
  phase-measure (ps) (label) =
   let hwm = __heap-save
   in let deck-now = __deck-pos
   in deck-record (PhaseMetrics {
    label = label,
    bivy-origin = ps.bivy-origin,
    bivy-hwm = hwm,
    deck-origin = ps.deck-origin,
    deck-end = deck-now
   })

 The write-path bound every guarded phase shares. A phase
 that reserves a deck passes its ceiling down to whichever loop writes
 that deck in bulk, and the loop asks this before each item: is there
 still a guard band's worth of room? A ceiling of zero means unbounded,
 which is what a caller with no reservation of its own passes.

 This is a WRITE-path check on purpose. check-deck-overflow runs after
 the phase, which is after a deck that grew into the bivy has already
 been read back -- so it reports damage rather than preventing it, and
 it cannot even do that reliably, because phase-measure's own record is
 allocated on the deck that overflowed.

  deck-short-of : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  deck-short-of (ceiling) (band) =
   if ceiling > 0 & __deck-pos + band >= ceiling then True else False

 There are two of these because there are two ways a phase writes its
 deck, and only one of them moves __deck-pos.

 __deck-pos reads the deck-pos cell. deck-record is compiled by
 emit-deck-record-wrapper into __deck-enter, the body, __deck-exit:
 enter copies the cell into R10 and exit writes R10 back, and the
 write-back happens only when the nesting counter returns to zero. So
 inside a phase-wide deck-record extent the cell is FROZEN at the
 deck's base for the whole phase, and every allocation in that extent
 moves R10 instead. A loop that sits inside such an extent and asks
 deck-short-of is asking whether base + band >= base + height -- a
 question about the FLOOR's width that never mentions the usage, true
 only when the floor is narrower than the band.

 Measured 2026-07-21: an in-loop probe through rewrite-ir-defs, over
 the 2.81 MB selfhost, printed __deck-pos as 0x168961a2 on every one
 of its iterations while the phase wrote 19.1 MB. Starving that floor
 to 16 MB did not raise CDX9002; it crashed in __text_compare on a
 garbage pointer, which is the failure the guard exists to prevent,
 with the guard present and compiled in.

 A phase whose deck writes go through MANY SMALL deck-record extents
 is the other case: each extent's exit returns the counter to zero and
 writes R10 back, so the cell tracks the frontier and deck-short-of is
 live. SCOPE is that shape (resolve-expr wraps per-pattern-variable
 results), which is why its guard works.

 Use deck-bound-short-of from inside a phase-wide extent and
 deck-short-of from outside one. Getting this backwards is silent in
 both directions: deck-short-of inside an extent never fires, and
 deck-bound-short-of outside one reads the bivy pointer, which is
 above every deck ceiling, and fires on the first item.

 Which one a loop is in is a fact about the CODE and not about the
 phase. RESOLVE, LIFT, LOWER and the PARSE-KEEP copy are all inside
 theirs and all read R10. CHECK is not, and nothing about CHECK's shape
 says so: check-chapter issues a bare __deck-exit immediately before
 check-all-defs and a __deck-enter after it, three lines apart, so the
 walk between them is bivy-bound and reads the cell. Look for that pair
 before choosing a predicate; assuming from the phase's name gets it
 wrong for exactly one phase, and wrong there is a compiler that
 type-checks one definition and emits nothing.

  deck-bound-short-of : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  deck-bound-short-of (ceiling) (band) =
   if ceiling > 0 & __heap-save + band >= ceiling then True else False

  phase-compact : Nothing = __heap-restore (__deck-pos)

  phase-compact-poison : Integer -> Nothing
  phase-compact-poison (byte) =
   let old-top = __heap-save
   in let new-top = __deck-pos
   in let restored = __heap-restore new-top
   in if old-top > new-top then __memset new-top byte (old-top - new-top)
      else restored

